note the problem section (#why)
text pw1 "<section class='prose' id='why'>\n<h2>The problem it fixes</h2>\n"
text pw2 "<p>Agents write “done” and “all tests pass” easily. The sentence may describe a different commit, a suite that was skipped, or a test that could never fail. Reading the report does not tell you which.</p>\n"
text pw3 "<p>INTERPLANE starts every completion claim as UNVERIFIED. Evidence has to name the exact revision, the task, the input and the checker that produced it. Missing, failed, mismatched or untrusted evidence is a HOLD, never a quiet pass. Under the rule, an unsupported claim goes back to the agent that made it, with the facts it still has to supply. Today forge decide and forge gate apply this rule in their test suites and in replays of real desk decisions; no runner applies them to live merges yet, and automatic interception of an agent's completion message is still being built, as listed below.</p>\n"
text pw4 "<p>The scope of a PASS is written down: the declared checks passed on that exact revision, and their negative control failed. A PASS is not proof that the software is correct in general, that a statement is true, or that a model meant what it said. Checks cover what they cover.</p>\n"
text pw5 "<blockquote>A completion is a claim. Evidence decides what it is worth.</blockquote>\n<h3>Two uses</h3>\n<ol>\n"
text pw6 "<li><strong>Code completion proof.</strong> A claim that a change is done is checked against receipts for the exact revision: which checks ran, their exit codes, and a negative control that had to fail. This is what forge decide and forge gate check today; the desk protocol still performs the merge.</li>\n"
text pw7 "<li><strong>Model-service performance over time.</strong> The same frozen task set, run against a model service again and again, to detect measured regressions on those tasks. Planned; <a href='#longitudinal'>what it can and cannot show</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n"

note what runs today (#modules)
text pm1 "<section id='modules'>\n<h2>What runs today, and what does not</h2>\n<p class='section-lede'>Each piece is labelled with the state its own source records. Planned work is listed as planned.</p>\n<ul class='stage-list'>\n"
text pm2 "<li><span class='pill ok'>HOST TESTED</span> <strong>forge decide</strong>: decides MERGE or HOLD from three inputs: the patch's exact head, an independent reviewer's verdict for that head, and a gate receipt. Every fail-closed invariant has a test case that breaks only it and a mutant that removes it; the suite requires each mutant to be caught. "
text plsrc "Source"
text plrule "rule"
text pltests "tests"
text pm4 "<li><span class='pill ok'>HOST TESTED</span> <strong>forge gate</strong>: runs a repository's gate and its negative control in fresh clones of the exact head and writes a receipt with the commands, exit codes and log hashes. A run killed at its timeout is a HOLD, not a result.</li>\n"
text pm5 "<li><span class='pill wait'>LIMIT</span> <strong>Identities and receipts are not signed yet</strong>: reviewer and author names are unsigned text, and the gate command is the one the repository declares. A receipt is evidence the forge wrote, not a cryptographic proof. Signed verdicts and receipts are planned. No gate receipts are published on this site yet.</li>\n"
text pm6 "<li><span class='pill wait'>IN PROGRESS</span> <strong>Completion interception</strong>: no agent runtime I use stops an agent's “done” automatically today. A claim gate that returns an unsupported completion to the producing agent, with the missing facts and a bounded number of repair attempts, is being built. It is not merged and not deployed.</li>\n"
text pm7 "<li><span class='pill none'>PLANNED</span> <strong>Longitudinal model checks</strong>: the same frozen tasks, measured over time. <a href='#longitudinal'>How, and what it cannot show</a>.</li>\n"
text pm8 "<li><span class='pill none'>PROPOSED</span> <strong>J-Space</strong>: an isolated verification workspace whose English is rendered only from the verifier's payload. <a href='#j-space'>Design and limits</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n"
text pm9 "<p class='fine'>The forge web app that serves <a href='https://interplane.io/explore'>interplane.io/explore</a> is open source too: <a href='https://radicle.network/nodes/rosa.radicle.network/rad:z3KFmLGeYdvDAgwpCgdBd1ENW7nU5'>interplane-forge on Radicle</a>.</p>\n"
text pm10 "<h3>Protocol modules underneath</h3>\n<p class='section-lede'>The forge applies INTERPLANE's protocol rule, that a request is not authority, to code merges. The protocol's own modules:</p>\n<ol class='modules'>\n"
text pmo1 "<li><h3>Core</h3><p class='question'>What crosses the line?</p><p>The shared vocabulary: capabilities, requests, results, traces and the canonical byte form every digest is computed over.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmo2 "<li><h3>Lenshift</h3><p class='question'>What did the model actually say?</p><p>Reads each model's native tool-call dialect into one form. An incomplete or ambiguous call is rejected, never guessed.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmo3 "<li><h3>CrossAxis</h3><p class='question'>Which tools does this task need?</p><p>Selects the few tools a task needs from a runtime's catalog, so a small local model is not buried under schemas it will never use.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmo4 "<li><h3>Crossveil</h3><p class='question'>Who is allowed to act?</p><p>Tracks where every piece of text came from. Content a model read on the web cannot grant authority, and an effect it asks for while untrusted text is in view is held for a person.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmo5 "<li><h3>Probe</h3><p class='question'>What does this model and backend really support?</p><p>Live probes that measure a model, backend and runtime combination instead of trusting its documentation.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmo6 "<li><h3>Vectorveil</h3><p class='question'>How do bytes travel?</p><p>The transport envelope. Specified; the implementation belongs to the 0.4 Execution stage.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmo7 "<li><h3>RelayLine</h3><p class='question'>How does a long call stream?</p><p>Streaming, cancellation and continuation. The contract is written; the implementation belongs to 0.4.</p><a class='spec' href='"
text pmoz "'>Specification</a></li>\n"
text pm11 "</ol>\n"

note measuring over time (#longitudinal)
text pl1 "<section class='prose' id='longitudinal'>\n<h2>Measuring model behavior over time</h2>\n"
text pl2 "<p><span class='pill none'>PLANNED</span> A product direction. No runner exists yet, and nothing on this page starts a run.</p>\n"
text pl3 "<p>Checking one completion and measuring a model are different jobs. A completion check asks whether this change, at this revision, passed its declared checks. A longitudinal check asks whether a model, under the name a provider serves it, still does as well on the same tasks as it did before.</p>\n"
text pl4 "<p>The method: freeze a set of tasks, their inputs and their checker; run them against a model on a schedule; record the date, the model name as served and every score; compare each run with a recorded baseline. A drop on those tasks is measured drift on those tasks. It does not show why the model changed, whether anyone intended it, or whether a renamed model is a different one.</p>\n"
text pl5 "<p>No historical launch baseline is available here for any model, so this page makes no claim that any model has degraded. Published precedent for the method: Chen, Zaharia and Zou measured the March and June 2023 versions of GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 on the same tasks and recorded large changes in behavior (<a href='https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09009'>arXiv:2307.09009</a>). That is evidence about those versions, not about any other model.</p>\n"

note J-Space (#j-space)
text pj1 "<section class='prose' id='j-space'>\n<h2>J-Space: an isolated verification workspace</h2>\n"
text pj2 "<p><span class='pill none'>PROPOSED</span> A design, not a service. Public deployment has not run, there is no public endpoint, and nothing on this page starts it.</p>\n"
text pj3 "<p>The proposal keeps two jobs apart. An isolated verifier checks a claim against its declared evidence and produces a verdict payload. A separate renderer turns only that payload into plain English: a fixed system prompt and the payload, with no tools and no conversation history, so the only facts it is given are the ones the verifier produced. A model can still misword them, so the payload stays the record; the English is a reading of it, not new evidence.</p>\n"
text pj4 "<p>No J-Space source is published yet, so there is nothing to link as its implementation. Related foundation, not J-Space itself: the forge verification "
text pj5 " and "
text plsource "source"
text pj6 ". This is not an inference service and nothing here sells model access.</p>\n"

note the 0.1 receipt (#receipt). The numbers are the hand constants of page.rs, read there from
